  WHY DEFERRED (full-app Xcode gate — ZERO PrivacySection errors; every blocker pre-exists this change):
    These two were EXECUTED on this slot (unique per-run log filenames + an isolated -derivedDataPath, because the
    shared /tmp meant the first run's default log was overwritten by a concurrent sibling slot — a shared-runner
    artifact, not a build fact). The Shared.xcframework staged successfully for this slot; the failures are:
    iOS  : the app target fails to resolve `import XCTest` — 4× "Unable to resolve module dependency: 'XCTest'"
           in target 'TeslaSync' (e.g. AcDcStatsPanel.Tests.swift:19, … and this surface's PrivacySection.Tests
           / .AdapterTests). Root cause is the repo-wide composition: project.yml adds the WHOLE
           TeslaSync/feature-views directory — which holds 175+ co-located *.Tests.swift across peer surfaces —
           to the APP target's sources, and XCTest is not available to an iOS-sim app target. The slot-330
           project.pbxproj confirms PrivacySection.Tests.swift is a member of both APP targets' Sources phases
           (the same convention every P4 feature-view ships with). grep of the slot-330 iOS log for
           "PrivacySection*.swift:NN:CC: error" = 0 hits — the surface itself has no compile error.
    macOS: the build is gated at the development-signing check — the ONLY two errors are
           "\"TeslaSync-macOS\" has entitlements that require signing with a development certificate" and the
           same for "TeslaSyncWidgets-macOS" (these targets set CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED + ENABLE_HARDENED_RUNTIME,
           which this runner cannot satisfy without a provisioned dev cert; ad-hoc signing is incompatible with
           the hardened runtime). XCTest resolves for macOS app targets, so it is NOT the iOS blocker here; grep
           of the macOS log for any PrivacySection compile error = 0 hits (the surface's compilation is proven
           independently by the SwiftPM harness + the iOS 18 SDK typecheck above).
    Both blockers are exactly the artifacts apps/macos-pending-verifications.md defers to the ADR-012 signed
    macos-latest CI runner, and the *.Tests.swift → test-target re-homing is owned by the P4/P99 Apple acceptance
    gate (a project.yml source-membership change), NOT a single feature-view prompt (task rule: do not fix
    pre-existing unrelated breakage). The shipped surface is INDEPENDENTLY proven to compile (SwiftPM harness +
    iOS 18 SDK typecheck) and its 38 tests pass. This matches the sanctioned disposition of every prior P4
    feature-view (cf. 0088 LoadingSkeleton, 0010 ToolCard, 0003 ClientUtilitiesSection).

